{
  "id": "COMPOUND:118 - PROTEIN:970",
  "verbalization": "Spermine is implicated in the regulation of Penicillin-binding protein 1A (PBP1A) through multiple pathways, with the core mechanism involving the targeting of sodium channel proteins. The evidence suggests that Spermine acts as a modulator of PBP1A by influencing the activity of sodium channel protein type 11 subunit alpha, which in turn is targeted by compounds like 1,2,3,4-tetramethylbenzene and fuscaxanthone C. These compounds, in turn, exert their effects on PBP1A, indicating a potential role in the regulation of bacterial cell wall synthesis. The presence of Spermine as a target of multiple compounds that ultimately affect PBP1A suggests a broader regulatory network, possibly involving ion channel modulation and subsequent downstream effects on bacterial physiology. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Spermine to influence PBP1A through a complex interplay of ion channel activity and antibiotic resistance mechanisms.",
